    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) They are relative major and minor: A minor is the relative minor of C major, its tonic lying on the sixth degree of the C major scale. Both use the same notes (C, D, E, F, G, A, B), so both carry no sharps or flats.\nWhy they sound different: the difference is not which notes are used but which note is the centre. C major takes C as tonic and phrases tend to settle on it, and C to its third E is a major third, giving a bright colour. A minor takes A as tonic, and A to its third C is a minor third, giving a darker one.\nShifting the tonic changes the relation of every other note to the centre, so the same material produces a different tonal sense. In practice the minor commonly raises its seventh degree (G sharp here) to form a leading note and strengthen the pull back to the tonic, at which point the two no longer use identical notes.\n(b) C–E is a major 3rd; C–F a perfect 4th; C–G a perfect 5th.\nTwo steps: first, count the number of letter names from lower to upper inclusive — C, D, E gives a 3rd; C to G spans five letter names, a 5th. This step ignores accidentals. Second, determine quality by counting the semitones between the notes and comparing with the standard for that number. Thirds are major (4 semitones) or minor (3); unisons, fourths, fifths and octaves are perfect and admit augmented and diminished but not major and minor.\nThe steps must stay in order: C to D sharp and C to E flat sound alike, but the first is an augmented 2nd and the second a minor 3rd, because the letter count differs.",
    "contentEn": "(a) State the relationship between C major and A minor, and explain why they share a key signature yet sound different.\n(b) Name the intervals C–E, C–F and C–G (including quality), and set out the two steps for identifying an interval.",
    "explanationEn": "The key in (a) is that the difference lies in the centre, not the material. \"One is major and one is minor\" restates the question; the answer must say how shifting the tonic changes every note’s relation to the centre. The closing note on the raised seventh also earns credit, since it marks the limit of saying that relative keys share a signature.\nThe insistence on order in (b) exists because enharmonic spelling is the standard trap in theory: two intervals that sound alike can have entirely different names. Counting letters before semitones avoids it, and giving the C–D sharp against C–E flat example is stronger than an abstract statement."
