    "year": 0,
    "content": "（a）在 C 大調上寫出 I、IV、V 三個三和弦所包含的音。\n（b）說明何以「V 至 I」是最強的終止式，並指出其中哪一個音起關鍵作用。\n（c）指出「IV 至 I」的終止式名稱，並說明其聽感何以較為柔和。",
    "explanation": "（b）要求指出關鍵音，這一點值得留意 —— 許多答案能說出「V 至 I 最強」，卻答不出何以如此。導音的半音傾向是整個西方功能和聲的核心動力，而它亦解釋了為何小調常須升高第七級：自然小音階的第七級距離主音為全音，傾向不足，須人為升高方能構成有效的終止式。\n（c）的比較令兩者的分別更清晰：正格終止有導音、有緊張與解決的過程；變格終止無導音、有共同音、變化平順。把「解決」與「安放」分開表述，比只寫「比較柔和」更能顯示理解其成因。\n作答此類題目時，養成先寫出和弦所含各音的習慣 —— 一旦音名列出，導音是否在內、有無共同音等問題便一目了然，毋須憑記憶。",
    "referenceAnswer": "（a）在 C 大調上：\nI（主和弦）= C、E、G\nIV（下屬和弦）= F、A、C\nV（屬和弦）= G、B、D\n\n（b）V 至 I（正格終止，authentic cadence）之所以最強：\n關鍵在於 V 和弦所包含的 B 音 —— 它是 C 大調的第七級，即【導音】。導音與主音之間只相隔半音，此一極近的距離產生強烈的向上傾向，聽感上要求解決至 C。\n此外，V 和弦另有一音 D，為第二級，傾向下行解決至主音 C；而根音 G 至 C 為完全四度上行（或完全五度下行），此為最穩固的低音進行。\n三重傾向同時指向主和弦 —— 導音上行、第二級下行、低音作四五度跳進 —— 故 V 至 I 產生最明確的結束感。\n若必須指出一個關鍵音，答案為【B（導音）】：正是它的半音傾向令此終止式無可替代。\n\n（c）IV 至 I 稱為變格終止（plagal cadence），因常見於聖詠末尾的「阿們」而俗稱阿們終止。\n聽感較柔和的原因：\nIV 和弦（F、A、C）並不包含導音 B，故缺少了那個要求解決至主音的半音傾向。其低音由 F 進行至 C，屬完全五度上行（或完全四度下行），穩固而不緊迫。\n此外，IV 和弦已經包含主音 C 本身，兩個和弦之間有共同音，變化幅度較小，故過渡平順。\n結果是：變格終止同樣具有結束感，但那是一種「安放」而非「解決」—— 沒有先製造緊張再化解的過程，故聽來安詳而非有力。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) In C major: I = C, E, G; IV = F, A, C; V = G, B, D.\n(b) V–I, the authentic cadence, is strongest because of the B in the dominant chord — the seventh degree of C major, the leading note. It lies a semitone below the tonic, and that proximity creates a strong upward pull demanding resolution to C.\nThe chord also contains D, the second degree, which tends downward to the tonic, while the bass moves G to C, a rising perfect fourth (or falling fifth), the most stable of bass movements.\nThree tendencies point to the tonic at once — leading note rising, second degree falling, bass leaping by fourth or fifth — giving the clearest sense of closure. If one note must be named, it is B, the leading note: its semitone pull is what makes the cadence irreplaceable.\n(c) IV–I is the plagal cadence, popularly the \"amen\" cadence from its use at the end of hymns.\nIt sounds gentler because IV (F, A, C) contains no leading note and so lacks the semitone tendency demanding resolution. Its bass moves F to C, a rising perfect fifth or falling fourth, stable but not urgent. The IV chord also already contains the tonic C, so the two chords share a note and the change is small, making the transition smooth.\nThe plagal cadence therefore closes as well, but by settling rather than resolving: there is no tension created and then discharged, so it sounds serene rather than emphatic.",
    "contentEn": "(a) Give the notes of the I, IV and V triads in C major.\n(b) Explain why V–I is the strongest cadence and which note is decisive.\n(c) Name the IV–I cadence and explain why it sounds gentler.",
    "explanationEn": "Part (b) asks which note is decisive, and this deserves attention: many answers can say V–I is strongest without saying why. The semitone pull of the leading note is the central engine of Western functional harmony, and it also explains why the minor commonly raises its seventh — in the natural minor the seventh sits a whole tone below the tonic, too weak a pull to form an effective cadence without alteration.\nThe comparison in (c) sharpens the contrast: the authentic cadence has a leading note and a cycle of tension and release; the plagal has none, shares a note, and changes little. Distinguishing settling from resolving shows more understanding than calling it gentler.\nFor questions of this kind, write out the notes of each chord first — once the pitches are listed, whether the leading note is present and whether the chords share notes can be read off rather than recalled."
